-- title:
--   Formal Čerednik–Drinfeld quotient property at tower level ℓ
-- statement:
--   The setting is as follows. Fixed are primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, and a squarefree natural number $N \neq 0$ divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$.
--
--   Arithmetic data at $r$: a characteristic-zero discrete valuation domain $\mathcal O$ in which $2$ is a unit, with an irreducible element $\pi$ such that $\mathcal O$ is $\pi$-adically complete, the residue ring $\mathcal O/(\pi)$ has exactly $r$ elements and $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$; a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ realised as the fraction field of $\mathcal O$; and an $\mathcal O$-algebra $Onr$ which is a characteristic-zero domain, complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, with that ideal maximal, every element of $Onr$ satisfying a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo that ideal (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $Onr$ having a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`), together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $Fr$ of $Onr$ with $Fr(x) \equiv x^{r}$ modulo $(\pi)$. Further, a homomorphism $vdet : GL_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) which, by `hvdet`, computes the $\pi$-adic valuation of the determinant: $vdet(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\pi^{n}$ for some $u \in \mathcal O^{\times}$.
--
--   Quaternionic and moduli data: rationals $a,b$ such that $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$ is indefinite ($0 < a$ or $0 < b$) and the completion at a finite place $v$ of $\mathbb Q$ is a division algebra exactly when $v$ lies above $r$ or above $\bar r$ (`hB`); a maximal order $\Lambda$ in it; a scheme $\mathcal X$ with a morphism $f$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and a point-assignment $pt$ sending a fake elliptic curve with level structure $N$ over an $\mathcal O$-algebra $S$ to a section of $f$ over $\operatorname{Spec} S$, such that $(\mathcal X,f,pt)$ is a coarse moduli space in the sense of `IsCoarseModuli` (invariance under isomorphisms, compatibility with pullback, bijectivity on algebraically closed fields, and the universal property); the generic fibre $\mathcal X \times_{\mathcal O} K_0$ is assumed geometrically reduced and geometrically connected.
--
--   Full-level and group data: an integer $n \geq 3$ coprime to $N$ and divisible by neither $r$ nor $\bar r$; a fine moduli scheme $M$ over $\mathcal O$ for pairs (fake elliptic curve, full level-$n$ structure) with point-assignment $ptF$ (`IsFineModuli`); a group $G$ acting by automorphisms of $M$ through $\rho$, with labels $\chi : G \to \Lambda$, such that $\rho$ is a level-twisting action in the sense of `IsLevelTwistAction` (each $\rho(h)$ is over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$, twisting the level structure by $\chi(h)$ moves $ptF$ by $\rho(h)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, injective and surjective modulo $n$); the forgetful morphism $p : M \to \mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$, $G$-invariant and compatible with the point-assignments.
--
--   Hecke tower data: for each prime $\ell$ different from $r$ and $\bar r$, a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\mathcal O$ with point-assignment $ptT$ on pairs (fake elliptic curve, extra level at $\ell$) making it a coarse moduli space in the sense of `IsCoarseModuliT`, together with the two degeneracy morphisms $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) : \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$, characterised on points by forgetting the extra level ($hd_0$) and by passing to the $\ell$-isogenous curve ($hd_1$, via `FakeEllipticCurve.IsLevelIsogeny`); and two Atkin–Lehner involutions $ar$, $arbar$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\mathcal O$ realising the Atkin–Lehner quotient at $r$ and at $\bar r$ on points.
--
--   Definite-side data: rationals $a_1,b_1$ with $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ definite ($a_1<0$, $b_1<0$) and ramified exactly at $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$ containing an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$; an adelic unit $n_1$ in the prime Hecke set of $R_1$ at $r$ such that $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ is an Eichler order of level $Nr$; an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra map $\iota_0 : \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$; a finite place $v$ above $r$; the subgroup $\Gamma t$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^{\times}$ consisting of the units integral at all places away from $v$ ([`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37)); for each tower prime $\ell$ elements $s(\ell)$ of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^{\times}$ and $sf(\ell)$ adelically, satisfying `hs`: $sf(\ell)$ has component $s(\ell)$ at every place not above $r$ and component $1$ above $r$, the product of the diagonal image of $\ell$ with $sf(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in the level Hecke set `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` when $\ell \mid N$ and in the prime Hecke set of $R_1 \cap n_1R_1n_1^{-1}$ at $\ell$ otherwise, and the reduced norm of $s(\ell)$ is $\ell$; the subgroups $\Gamma t(\ell) = \Gamma t \cap s(\ell)\Gamma t\, s(\ell)^{-1}$; an element $wbar$ of reduced norm $\bar r$ normalising $\Gamma t$; a homomorphism $\theta t : \Gamma t \to G$.
--
--   Uniformisation data at the bottom of the tower: families $\Theta_f$ and $\Theta_T(\ell)$ assigning, to every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map from $(Onr\text{-points}) \times \Omega(K_0,\pi)(B) \times G$ — respectively from $(Onr\text{-points}) \times \Omega(K_0,\pi)(B)$ — to the $B$-points of $M$ over $\mathcal O$ (respectively of $\mathcal Y_\ell$); here `AlgFunctor.corep Onr` is $B \mapsto \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal O\text{-alg}}(Onr,B)$, `Omega K₀ π` is Drinfeld's functor of Deligne data on the lattice tree, and `AlgFunctor.const G` is the constant functor $G$. These are required to satisfy: naturality in $B$ (`hnat`); $G$-equivariance, $\Theta_f(x, gh)$ pushed by $\rho(h)$ equals $\Theta_f(x,g)$ (`hG`); $\Gamma t$-invariance, namely if $x'$ is the twisted translate of $x$ by $\iota_0(\gamma)$ in the sense of `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` (first component twisted by $Fr^{-vdet}$, second component a pullback of Deligne data along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$), then $\Theta_f(x', \theta t(\gamma) g) = \Theta_f(x,g)$ (`hinv`); and formal étaleness (`het`): for every surjection $p : B \to B_0$ of $\mathcal O$-algebras with $\pi$ nilpotent whose kernel has square zero in the stated pointwise form, every $x_0$ over $B_0$ and every $B$-point $y$ of $M$ lifting $\Theta_f(x_0)$, there is a unique $x$ over $B$ with $p_*x = x_0$ and $\Theta_f(x) = y$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses the conclusion is the following statement, quantified over every tower prime $\ell$ (different from $r$, $\bar r$) and over every level-$\ell$ fine package, namely: a scheme $M_\ell$ with a morphism $f_{M_\ell}$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and an assignment $ptF_\ell$ on triples (fake elliptic curve, full level $n$, extra level at $\ell$) making it a fine moduli space in the sense of `IsFineModuliT`; morphisms $\pi_\ell : M_\ell \to M$ over $\mathcal O$ forgetting the extra level on points, and $p_\ell : M_\ell \to \mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\mathcal O$ forgetting the full level on points; an action $\rho_\ell$ of $G$ on $M_\ell$ over $\mathcal O$, commuting with $\pi_\ell$ in the sense $\rho_\ell(h) \text{ followed by } \pi_\ell = \pi_\ell \text{ followed by } \rho(h)$, leaving $p_\ell$ invariant, and twisting $ptF_\ell$ by $\chi(h)$ in the sense of `FakeEllipticCurve.WithFullLevel.IsTwistVia` together with matching of the extra-level subschemes on points ($h\rho_\ell tw$); a lift $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ of the uniformisation map to $M_\ell$ subject to naturality ($hnat_\ell$), $G$-equivariance ($hG_\ell$), $\Gamma t(\ell)$-invariance ($hinv_\ell$), formal étaleness ($het_\ell$), and the fibre description ($hfib_\ell$): for an algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent, $\Theta_{f,\ell}((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_{f,\ell}((\psi',P'),g')$ holds if and only if there is $\gamma \in \Gamma t$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma t(\ell)$, $g' = \theta t(\gamma) g$, $P'$ the pullback of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$ in the sense of `DeligneDatum.IsPullback`, and $\psi' = \psi \circ Fr^{-vdet(\iota_0(\gamma))}$ on all $y \in Onr$ fixed by the relevant Frobenius powers coming from central elements $z \in \Gamma t$ with $\theta t(z) = 1$; compatibility of $\Theta_{f,\ell}$ with $\Theta_f$ along $\pi_\ell$ ($h\pi_\ell\Theta$) and of $\Theta_{f,\ell}(\cdot,1)$ with $\Theta_T(\ell)$ along $p_\ell$ ($hp_\ell\Theta$); the requirement that $f_{M_\ell}$ be locally of finite type; and a presentation of $\mathcal Y_\ell$ as the quotient of $M_\ell$ by $\rho_\ell(G)$, that is, a scheme $X'_\ell$ with a $G$-invariant morphism $\pi'_\ell : M_\ell \to X'_\ell$ which is integral, affine and surjective on points, such that each $\pi'_\ell$-comorphism on opens is injective with image exactly the $\rho_\ell(G)$-invariant sections, invariant affine opens of $M_\ell$ descend to affine opens of $X'_\ell$, together with a structure morphism $\pi_{X'_\ell}$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ compatible with $f_{M_\ell}$ and an isomorphism $e_{X_\ell} : X'_\ell \to \mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\mathcal O$ with $\pi'_\ell$ followed by $e_{X_\ell}$ equal to $p_\ell$.
--
--   Given in addition the surjectivity hypothesis $hliftsurj_\ell$: for every algebraically closed $\mathcal O$-algebra field $k$ with $\pi$ nilpotent, every $\psi : Onr \to k$ and every $k$-point $y$ of $M_\ell$ over $\mathcal O$, there exist $P \in \Omega(K_0,\pi)(k)$ and $g \in G$ with $\Theta_{f,\ell}((\psi,P),g) = y$, the assertion is that $\Theta_T(\ell)$ is universal among $\Gamma t(\ell)$-invariant natural families. Precisely: for every scheme $T$ with a morphism $t$ to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and every family $\rho$ assigning to each $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ with $\pi$ nilpotent a map from $(Onr\text{-points}) \times \Omega(K_0,\pi)(B)$ to the $B$-points of $T$ over $\mathcal O$, which is natural in $B$ and invariant under the twisted action of every $\gamma \in \Gamma t(\ell)$ (if $x'$ is the twisted translate of $x$ by $\iota_0(\gamma)$ then $\rho(x') = \rho(x)$), there exists a family $u$ assigning to each such $B$ a map from the $B$-points of $\mathcal Y_\ell$ over $\mathcal O$ to the $B$-points of $T$, such that: $u$ is natural in $B$; $u \circ \Theta_T(\ell) = \rho$ on every such $B$; and $u$ is unique with these two properties, in the sense that any other such family $u'$ that is natural and satisfies $u' \circ \Theta_T(\ell) = \rho$ agrees with $u$ on every $B$-point of $\mathcal Y_\ell$ for every admissible $B$.
--
--   This is the level-$\ell$ analogue, one step up the Hecke tower, of the statement that the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation exhibits the $\pi$-adic formal completion of the relevant Shimura curve as a formal quotient: it says that the tower curve $\mathcal Y_\ell$, presented as the quotient of the fine level-$\ell$ moduli scheme $M_\ell$ by the level-twisting $G$-action, corepresents $\Gamma t(\ell)$-invariant natural families on $(Onr\text{-points}) \times \hat\Omega$ through $\Theta_T(\ell)$. It is used in assembling the full uniformisation of the tower with its degeneracy and Atkin–Lehner structure.
